    <!-- Dialog theme overlay: inherits correct dark/light text from Material3.
         MaterialAlertDialogBuilder reads colorOnSurface automatically — white in
         dark mode, near-black in light mode. No hardcoded colours needed.

         colorPrimary IS DELIBERATELY NOT SET HERE (T081). It used to be pinned to
         @color/ufm_primary_light, and that pin was the bug this removal fixes.

         ContextThemeWrapper builds a dialog's theme by copying the Activity's
         theme and THEN applying this style on top (setTo(base.getTheme()) then
         applyStyle(UFM.Dialog, true)). Anything named here therefore beats the
         Activity — including the Colorblind overlay ThemeHelper applied to it. So
         while the pin existed, a dialog's colorPrimary was teal no matter what
         the Activity behind it used: it hid the Colorblind palette from every
         dialog button (FR-22), and it disagreed with the Activity in the mode-off
         case too, where the base themes use @color/theme_primary (mobile) and
         @color/ufm_primary (TV).

         Verified, not assumed: ThemeOverlay.Material3.MaterialAlertDialog declares
         no colorPrimary in material 1.14.0, so with this item gone the value
         resolves from the Activity theme by ordinary inheritance. `?attr/colorPrimary`
         was rejected as the replacement — a self-referential attribute inside the
         very overlay that would define it has no well-defined resolution order,
         and this file is not the place to depend on one.

         Consequence, stated plainly: this changes dialog accents with the mode
         OFF (teal -> theme_primary), and that is intended. It is the one place
         this feature alters the mode-off appearance, and it is recorded as such
         rather than folded silently into the colour conversions. -->
    <style name="UFM.Dialog" parent="ThemeOverlay.Material3.MaterialAlertDialog" />

